IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `scja-core`

-- scja1-part2-court.l4: Supreme Court of Judicature Act 1969 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation in force from 1 July 2024.
-- LAYER 1, Part 2 "The Supreme Court", ss 3 to 7. The source is a plain-text rendering, not the official text.
-- Every name begins with the section it implements. Imports the keel's `scja-core` only.
-- s 6 (the seal) is machinery: the Chief Justice's power to prescribe the seal by notification in the Gazette (reason 2); no rule here.
-- JUST TRUE and JUST FALSE are answers the Act gives in its own words; NOTHING means the Act does not answer and is never FALSE.

§ `Part 2: The Supreme Court`

§§ `Section 3: Superior courts`

-- Plain gloss: is this court one the Act declares a superior court of record? The declaration names three courts and does not say "only".
-- src:448 | 3. It is declared that the General Division of the High Court, the
-- src:449 | Appellate Division of the High Court and the Court of Appeal are
-- src:450 | superior courts of record.
@ref s 3 (src:448-450)
GIVEN court IS A Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3 superior court of record: the court is declared a superior court of record` MEANS
    CONSIDER court
    WHEN `General Division` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Appellate Division` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `Court of Appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
    OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (list not closed): "It is declared that the General Division of the High Court, the Appellate Division of the High Court and the Court of Appeal are superior courts of record"

§§ `Section 4: Precedence`

-- The class of rank a Supreme Court Judge falls in: 1 the Chief Justice, 2 a Vice-President of the Court of Appeal, 3 another Justice of the Court of Appeal,
-- 4 the President of the Appellate Division (when the Chief Justice is not that President), 5 another Judge of the Appellate Division, 6 a Judge of the High Court.
-- 0 means "not ranked by s 4": a Judicial Commissioner, a Senior Judge or an International Judge is not a Supreme Court Judge (s 2).
-- src:456 | 4. The Supreme Court Judges rank in the following order:
-- src:458 | (a) the Chief Justice;
-- src:460 | (b) the Vice‑Presidents of the Court of Appeal according to the
-- src:461 | order of their appointments;
-- src:463 | (c) the Justices of the Court of Appeal (other than the
-- src:464 | Vice‑Presidents of the Court of Appeal) according to the
-- src:465 | order of their appointments;
-- src:467 | (d) the President of the Appellate Division (if the Chief Justice
-- src:468 | is not the President of the Appellate Division);
-- src:470 | (e) the Judges of the Appellate Division (other than the
-- src:471 | President of the Appellate Division) according to the order
-- src:472 | of their appointments;
-- src:474 | (f) the Judges of the High Court according to the order of their
-- src:475 | appointments.
@ref s 4 (src:456-475)
GIVEN j IS A Judge
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` MEANS
    CONSIDER j's office
    WHEN `Chief Justice` THEN 1 -- (a)
    WHEN `Vice-President of the Court of Appeal` THEN 2 -- (b)
    WHEN `Justice of the Court of Appeal` THEN 3 -- (c)
    WHEN `President of the Appellate Division` THEN 4 -- (d)
    WHEN `Judge of the Appellate Division` THEN 5 -- (e)
    WHEN `Judge of the High Court` THEN 6 -- (f)
    OTHERWISE 0

-- Plain gloss: does the first Supreme Court Judge rank before the second? Within classes (b), (c), (e) and (f) "according to the order of their appointments";
-- two Judges appointed on one day, or two holders of the single office in (a) or (d), are not ordered by the Act (fork K3 of the keel).
@ref s 4(b), (c), (e), (f) the order of their appointments (src:460-475)
GIVEN first IS A Judge
      second IS A Judge
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 4 the first Judge ranks before the second` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` first EQUALS 0 THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): s 4 ranks Supreme Court Judges only
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` second EQUALS 0 THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): s 4 ranks Supreme Court Judges only
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` first LESS THAN `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` second THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` first GREATER THAN `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` second THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "rank in the following order"
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` first EQUALS 1 THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): one Chief Justice
        IF `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` first EQUALS 4 THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): one President of the Appellate Division
        IF first's `date of appointment` LESS THAN second's `date of appointment` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF first's `date of appointment` GREATER THAN second's `date of appointment` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "according to the order of their appointments"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Act does not order two Judges appointed on the same day

-- Plain gloss: of the Supreme Court Judges in this list, which one ranks before all the others? Seam 12 of PLAN.md: ss 30(3)(a) and 48(2)(a) (scja2) ask this.
-- A Judge in the list whom s 4 does not rank is passed over; no Judge, or a tie at the top, is NOTHING.
@ref s 4 rank in the following order (src:456-475)
GIVEN judges IS A LIST OF Judge
GIVETH A MAYBE Judge
`s 4 the most senior Supreme Court Judge among those sitting` MEANS
    CONSIDER top
    WHEN EMPTY THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): no ranked Judge, or none that ranks before all the others (a tie)
    WHEN j FOLLOWED BY rest THEN
        IF count top EQUALS 1 THEN JUST j ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): two Judges neither of whom the Act ranks before the other
    WHERE
        ranked MEANS filter (GIVEN c YIELD `s 4 the class of rank of the Judge, or 0 if s 4 does not rank the Judge` c GREATER THAN 0) judges
        top MEANS filter (GIVEN c YIELD all (GIVEN o YIELD o's `reference for this person` EQUALS c's `reference for this person` OR `s 4 the first Judge ranks before the second` c o EQUALS JUST TRUE) ranked) ranked

§§ `Section 5: Acting appointment`

-- A Judge who may stand in for the Chief Justice, with the two facts s 5(1) turns on.
DECLARE `s 5 Judge available to act` HAS
    judge IS A Judge
    `present in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN
    `able to act` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Plain gloss (s 5(1)): does the next Judge in precedence exercise the Chief Justice's powers now? "Whenever ... the Chief Justice is unable": a "whenever" is read as "where" (ruling of 2026-11-05).
-- src:481 | 5.—(1) Whenever during any period, owing to illness or absence
-- src:482 | from Singapore or any other cause, the Chief Justice is unable to
-- src:483 | exercise the powers or perform the duties of his or her office, such
-- src:484 | powers are to be had and may be exercised and such duties are to be
-- src:485 | performed by the Judge having precedence next after the Chief
-- src:486 | Justice, who is present in Singapore and able to act during that period.
@ref s 5(1) (src:481-486)
GIVEN `the Chief Justice is unable to exercise the powers or perform the duties of the office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Chief Justice unable, owing to illness or absence from Singapore or any other cause, to exercise the powers or perform the duties of the office?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(1) the powers and duties of the Chief Justice pass to the next Judge in precedence` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the Chief Justice is unable to exercise the powers or perform the duties of the office` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Whenever during any period, owing to illness or absence from Singapore or any other cause, the Chief Justice is unable"

-- Plain gloss (s 5(1)): which Judge exercises the Chief Justice's powers? The Judge having precedence next after the Chief Justice who is present in Singapore and able to act.
-- A Judge is a person, so this cannot carry JUST FALSE: the yes/no twin above says whether the rule applies (assumed, not ruled, per the brief).
@ref s 5(1) the Judge having precedence next after the Chief Justice, who is present in Singapore and able to act (src:483-486)
GIVEN `the Chief Justice is unable to exercise the powers or perform the duties of the office` IS A BOOLEAN
      candidates IS A LIST OF `s 5 Judge available to act`
GIVETH A MAYBE Judge
`s 5(1) the Judge who exercises the powers and duties of the Chief Justice` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`s 5(1) the powers and duties of the Chief Justice pass to the next Judge in precedence` `the Chief Justice is unable to exercise the powers or perform the duties of the office` EQUALS JUST TRUE) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the rule does not apply; the Chief Justice is not unable
        OTHERWISE `s 4 the most senior Supreme Court Judge among those sitting` (map (GIVEN c YIELD c's judge) (filter (GIVEN c YIELD c's `present in Singapore` AND c's `able to act` AND NOT (c's judge's office EQUALS `Chief Justice`)) candidates))

-- Plain gloss (s 5(2)): temporary absence in any part of Malaysia is not absence from Singapore for s 5. Any other absence is left to the ordinary meaning of the words, which s 5 does not define.
-- src:488 | (2) For the purposes of this section, temporary absence in any part
-- src:489 | of Malaysia is not to be deemed to be absence from Singapore.
@ref s 5(2) (src:488-489)
GIVEN `the absence is temporary and in a part of Malaysia` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the absence only a temporary absence in some part of Malaysia?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5(2) the absence counts as absence from Singapore for s 5` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the absence is temporary and in a part of Malaysia` THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "temporary absence in any part of Malaysia is not to be deemed to be absence from Singapore"
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "absence from Singapore" is not otherwise defined

§§ `Section 5A: International Judges`

-- A sitting of a Judge: the court, and the two facts s 5A turns on.
DECLARE `s 5A sitting` HAS
    court IS A Court
    `an appeal from a decision of the Singapore International Commercial Court` IS A BOOLEAN
    `an application relating to such an appeal` IS A BOOLEAN

-- Plain gloss: may this International Judge sit here? "may only sit in" the SICC, in an appeal from its decisions, and in an application relating to such an appeal. Other Judges: the section does not speak of them.
-- src:498 | 5A. An International Judge may only sit in the Singapore
-- src:499 | International Commercial Court, in an appeal from a decision of
-- src:500 | that Court, and in an application relating to such an appeal.
@ref s 5A (src:498-500)
GIVEN j IS A Judge
      sitting IS A `s 5A sitting`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 5A an International Judge may sit in this sitting` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (j's office EQUALS `International Judge`) THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "An International Judge may only sit" speaks of International Judges
        IF sitting's court EQUALS `Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF sitting's `an appeal from a decision of the Singapore International Commercial Court` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF sitting's `an application relating to such an appeal` THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may only sit in the Singapore International Commercial Court, in an appeal from a decision of that Court, and in an application relating to such an appeal"

§§ `Section 7: Vacations`

-- Plain gloss: do the vacations the Chief Justice specifies for a year stay within the limit? The number of months is a calendar month (Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1): not staged; taken as the number stated).
-- src:514 | 7. The Chief Justice may specify vacations of the Supreme Court,
-- src:515 | which must not exceed 2 months in any year.
@ref s 7 (src:514-515)
GIVEN `months of vacation specified in the year` IS A NUMBER @desc How many months in total are the vacations of the Supreme Court that the Chief Justice has specified for the year?
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 7 the vacations specified for the year are within the limit` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `months of vacation specified in the year` AT MOST 2 THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "which must not exceed 2 months in any year"
